<article class="env-lemma" id="01QF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01QF">Lemma <span data-tag="01QF">28.28.3</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. Suppose that there exist finitely many elements $f_1, \ldots , f_ n \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
each $X_{f_ i}$ is an affine open of $X$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the ideal generated by $f_1, \ldots , f_ n$ in $\Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ is equal to the unit ideal. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $X$ is affine. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume we have $f_1, \ldots , f_ n$ as in the lemma. We may write $1 = \sum g_ i f_ i$ for some $g_ j \in \Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)$ and hence it is clear that $X = \bigcup X_{f_ i}$. (The $f_ i$'s cannot all vanish at a point.) Since each $X_{f_ i}$ is quasi-compact (being affine) it follows that $X$ is quasi-compact. Hence we see that $X$ is quasi-affine by Lemma <a href="/tag/01QE" data-tag="01QE">28.28.1</a> above. Consider the open immersion 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  j : X \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)),  \]
</div>
    <p>
       see Lemma <a href="/tag/01P9" data-tag="01P9">28.19.4</a>. The inverse image of the standard open $D(f_ i)$ on the right hand side is equal to $X_{f_ i}$ on the left hand side and the morphism $j$ induces an isomorphism $X_{f_ i} \cong D(f_ i)$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01P8" data-tag="01P8">28.19.3</a>. Since the $f_ i$ generate the unit ideal we see that $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\Gamma (X, \mathcal{O}_ X)) = \bigcup _{i = 1, \ldots , n} D(f_ i)$. Thus $j$ is an isomorphism. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>